WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.496 --> 00:00:17.860
Ned's cry, everyone onboard the Abraham Lincoln rushed to get a look at the creature.

2
00:00:18.661 --> 00:00:20.903
Captain Farragut gave the order for the ship to stop.

3
00:00:21.804 --> 00:00:25.927
It was quite dark by then, so I wondered how Ned had been able to spot anything in

4
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:26.347
the water.

5
00:00:27.228 --> 00:00:31.372
But he had not been mistaken because we could all see the object that he was pointing to.

6
00:00:32.372 --> 00:00:36.193
About twelve hundred feet away, the sea seemed to be glowing.

7
00:00:37.073 --> 00:00:42.555
The creature gave off the intense mysterious light that several ships' captains had mentioned in their reports.

8
00:00:43.555 --> 00:00:47.656
The immense oval of light condensed at the center into a blazing core.

9
00:00:47.676 --> 00:00:52.778
"Oh, that's only some phosphorescent sea animals," one officer said.

10
00:00:52.798 --> 00:00:55.758
"No, sir, certainly not," I replied.

11
00:00:55.778 --> 00:01:01.520
"No sea animal ever produced such a powerful light; it's almost like an electric light.

12
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:05.281
Besides, that creature is headed straight for us!"

13
00:01:06.721 --> 00:01:08.262
A cry of alarm went up from the ship.

14
00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:11.102
"Silence!" roared Captain Farragut.

15
00:01:11.122 --> 00:01:12.743
"Reverse the engines."

16
00:01:13.743 --> 00:01:18.924
Our ship moved away from the burning light, but the supernatural animal came after us at twice our

17
00:01:19.024 --> 00:01:20.945
speed. Everyone gasped.

18
00:01:21.825 --> 00:01:25.966
More surprised than fearful, we stood there unable to speak or move.

19
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:29.007
The animal gained on us and circled the ship.

20
00:01:29.787 --> 00:01:34.077
Then it moved two or three miles away, trailing a glowing wake.

21
00:01:35.117 --> 00:01:39.899
Suddenly the creature rushed toward the Abraham Lincoln at an alarming rate of speed.

22
00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:44.801
It stopped about twenty feet from our hull and the light died out.

23
00:01:45.761 --> 00:01:50.183
The animal reappeared on the other side of the vessel as if it had slid under our hull.

24
00:01:51.163 --> 00:01:53.564
At any moment a fatal collision might have occurred.

25
00:01:54.245 --> 00:01:58.506
But instead of attacking the creature, we fled—a maneuver that amazed me.

26
00:01:59.627 --> 00:02:05.594
Usually Captain Farragut displayed no emotion, but at that point he looked completely astonished.

27
00:02:05.614 --> 00:02:12.498
"Professor Aronnax, I don't know what kind of formidable creature I'm dealing with," he said as if he

28
00:02:12.598 --> 00:02:14.559
sensed that I was questioning his actions.

29
00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:17.460
"I won't risk my ship in this darkness.

30
00:02:17.841 --> 00:02:21.302
Besides, how can we attack this unknown thing when we can't see it?

31
00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:23.464
How can we defend ourselves from it?

32
00:02:23.824 --> 00:02:27.306
We must wait for daylight to attack this extraordinary narwhal."

33
00:02:28.166 --> 00:02:31.028
The crew members of the Abraham Lincoln were on their feet all night.

34
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.541
No one thought of sleep even though the ship slowed down.

35
00:02:35.541 --> 00:02:41.723
Imitating the warship, the creature let the waves rock it, but it seemed oddly determined not to leave

36
00:02:41.763 --> 00:02:42.203
the scene.

37
00:02:43.224 --> 00:02:48.585
Toward midnight, however, it disappeared, seeming to have died out like some large glowworm.

38
00:02:49.445 --> 00:02:50.026
Had it fled?

39
00:02:50.926 --> 00:02:56.508
At about one o'clock in the morning, we heard a deafening whistle like that produced by water rushing

40
00:02:56.548 --> 00:02:57.388
with great force.

41
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.982
The captain, Ned, and I were on the small deck at the stern of the ship, eagerly peering

42
00:03:04.322 --> 00:03:05.043
through the darkness.

43
00:03:05.063 --> 00:03:10.965
"Ned Land," said Captain Farragut, "you've often heard the roaring of whales, haven't you?"

44
00:03:10.985 --> 00:03:14.187
"Yes, sir, but never a whale like this.

45
00:03:14.647 --> 00:03:16.848
If only I could get close enough to harpoon it."

46
00:03:16.868 --> 00:03:22.210
"But to approach it, you'd need a small boat," said the captain, "and that would risk the lives

47
00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:22.810
of my men."

48
00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:26.172
"And my life too," Ned said quietly.

49
00:03:27.104 --> 00:03:30.986
Toward two o'clock in the morning, the bright light reappeared about five miles away.

50
00:03:31.926 --> 00:03:37.909
Despite the distance and the noise of the wind and the sea, we distinctly heard the loud sounds

51
00:03:38.009 --> 00:03:40.550
as the animal beat the surface of the water with its tail.

52
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.291
You could even hear its panting breath.

53
00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:47.152
We were on the alert and prepared for combat.

54
00:03:47.853 --> 00:03:52.855
While one of the officers loaded weapons, Ned contented himself with sharpening his harpoon.

55
00:03:53.895 --> 00:04:00.039
At six o'clock, day began to break; with the first glimmer of daylight, the beast's bright light disappeared.

56
00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:07.025
At seven o'clock, a very thick fog obscured our view, and the best telescopes could not penetrate it.

57
00:04:07.966 --> 00:04:10.848
That caused much disappointment and anger onboard the ship.

58
00:04:11.789 --> 00:04:15.832
I started to climb one of the masts; some of the officers were already above me.

59
00:04:16.733 --> 00:04:18.694
At eight o'clock, the fog began to lift.

60
00:04:19.735 --> 00:04:21.857
Suddenly Ned cried out, "Look!

61
00:04:22.417 --> 00:04:22.898
There it is!"

62
00:04:23.958 --> 00:04:25.680
Everyone turned toward where he pointed.

63
00:04:26.492 --> 00:04:31.493
A mile and a half from the ship, a long black body emerged a yard above the waves.

64
00:04:32.374 --> 00:04:35.275
The ship approached the beast and I examined it thoroughly.

65
00:04:36.215 --> 00:04:41.596
Prior reports had exaggerated its size; I estimated it to be no more than two hundred and fifty

66
00:04:41.656 --> 00:04:42.277
feet in length.

67
00:04:43.877 --> 00:04:49.959
While I observed the creature, it ejected two plumes of steam and water that reached a height of

68
00:04:50.199 --> 00:04:51.759
one hundred and twenty feet.

69
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:53.980
So that was how it breathed!

70
00:04:54.996 --> 00:04:59.837
I concluded that it must be a sea mammal of some kind, although one that was electric, like

71
00:04:59.857 --> 00:05:00.638
an electric eel.

72
00:05:01.658 --> 00:05:03.898
At last the moment for combat had arrived.

73
00:05:04.899 --> 00:05:08.720
Captain Farragut gave his orders, and the Abraham Lincoln went straight at the animal.

74
00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.281
The beast allowed the ship to come within three hundred feet.

75
00:05:13.261 --> 00:05:17.582
Then as if refusing to dive, it made a little turn and stopped.

76
00:05:18.442 --> 00:05:22.203
We pursued the creature like this for almost an hour without gaining a yard on it.

77
00:05:23.084 --> 00:05:23.684
"Well, Mr.

78
00:05:23.724 --> 00:05:28.421
Land," said the captain at last, "do you advise me to send out the small boats to capture

79
00:05:28.441 --> 00:05:28.821
the beast?"

80
00:05:29.842 --> 00:05:33.323
"No, sir, because we won't take this beast easily," replied Ned.

81
00:05:33.343 --> 00:05:35.643
"Increase our speed if you can, sir.

82
00:05:37.024 --> 00:05:40.025
I'll stand at the bow and throw my harpoon if I get the chance."

83
00:05:41.005 --> 00:05:45.506
The Abraham Lincoln sped up to eighteen and a half miles per hour, but the animal swam at

84
00:05:45.546 --> 00:05:46.326
that speed too.

85
00:05:47.227 --> 00:05:51.508
For a whole hour, the ship kept up this pace without gaining on the beast.

86
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.743
We increased our speed to above nineteen miles an hour, and so did the creature.

87
00:05:56.763 --> 00:05:58.404
"We will catch it!"

88
00:05:58.824 --> 00:06:01.806
cried Ned as the animal let us get a little closer.

89
00:06:02.747 --> 00:06:08.111
But just as Ned was about to throw his harpoon, the animal slipped away at a speed that

90
00:06:08.151 --> 00:06:12.894
was no less than thirty miles an hour A cry of fury broke out from everyone.

91
00:06:13.674 --> 00:06:17.557
It was noon and we were no closer to catching the beast than we had been at eight

92
00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:21.900
o'clock. Captain Farragut decided to fire the ship's cannons at the beast.

93
00:06:23.532 --> 00:06:27.055
One shot hit the animal but slid into the sea without hurting it.

94
00:06:27.936 --> 00:06:33.681
I wished the creature would wear itself out, but hours passed without its showing any signs of exhaustion.

95
00:06:34.642 --> 00:06:38.465
I don't know how far we traveled that day, but it must have been at least three hundred

96
00:06:38.505 --> 00:06:41.747
miles. Only darkness put an end to the chase.

97
00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:46.972
At a little before eleven o'clock, the electric light reappeared about three miles away.

98
00:06:48.013 --> 00:06:51.616
Rocking gently on the surface, the narwhal seemed to be sleeping.

99
00:06:52.448 --> 00:06:55.490
The ship approached it cautiously to avoid waking it.

100
00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:57.971
Ned was ready with his harpoon.

101
00:06:58.852 --> 00:07:04.134
No one dared to breathe; we were only about a hundred feet from the bright light.

102
00:07:05.075 --> 00:07:08.937
I saw Ned throw his harpoon and heard it strike a hard body.

103
00:07:09.938 --> 00:07:15.381
Suddenly the electric light went out, and two enormous gushes of water broke over the ship.

104
00:07:16.341 --> 00:07:21.224
Then the ship struck something and I was thrown over the railing and into the sea.
